Here is an example of a Perl program that downloads images from a specified URL:

```perl
use LWP::UserAgent;
use File::Basename;

# Initialize the LWP user agent
my $ua = LWP::UserAgent->new;

# Get the URL from the user
print "Enter the URL of the image: ";
my $url = <STDIN>;
chomp $url;

# Make the request
my $response = $ua->get($url);

# Check for errors
if ($response->is_error) {
    die "Error: " . $response->status_line;
}

# Get the filename from the URL
my ($filename) = fileparse($url, qr/\.[^.]*/);

# Save the image to a file
open(my $fh, '>', $filename) or die "Could not open file '$filename' $!";
print $fh $response->decoded_content;
close($fh);

print "Image downloaded successfully to $filename\n";
```

This program uses the LWP module to make the HTTP request and download the image. It gets the URL from the user, makes the request, extracts the filename from the URL, and saves the image to a file. If there is an error, the program will print an error message and exit.

Note that this is just an example and may not be suitable for all use cases. It is always a good idea to test your code thoroughly before using it in production.
